                <pb facs="0792" n="660" sameAs="frus:frus1926v01/pg660"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/7941: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >MacMurray</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1926-12-15T15:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">December 15,
                                1926—3 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received December 15—7:20
                        a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>615. Following from American consul general at Shanghai: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“December 14, 4 p.m. Fifteen hundred men being the vanguard Southern
                            forces have invaded Chekiang from Kiangsi and are advancing unopposed on
                            Hangchow. Declaration of independence of Chekiang momentarily expected.
                            If it is accompanied by expulsion of Southern[ers], it is believed Sun
                            Ch’uan-fang will acquiesce; otherwise, Sun expected to move loyal forces
                            into Chekiang to meet Southern [forces]. His forces on Chekiang border
                            rapidly being strengthened.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <p>It is understood that Southern Nationalists will not assent to making Chekiang a
                    buffer region but are determined to occupy it.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">MacMurray</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
